#!markdown

# Two line charges, live

A figure with controls. Move a slider and the kernel draws the figure again; drag a box on it to
zoom, shift-drag to pan, double-click to go back, and hover to read the position under the
pointer.

It needs the **Marey** extension in [Verso](https://github.com/DataficationSDK/Verso), and Verso
1.2.3 or newer, whose cells can talk back to the kernel. Anywhere else the same cells show the
figure with its controls disabled, and say that it is static.

#!fsharp

#r "nuget: Marey.Html"
#r "nuget: Marey.Fields"

open Marey
open Marey.UnitsOfMeasure

#!markdown

The potential of two line charges, +λ and −λ, a distance `d` apart: `λ / (2π ε₀) · ln(r₋ / r₊)`.
The compiler checks that this is a potential. A charge per length over a permittivity is coulombs
per farad, which is volts, so a `float<V>` is what comes out and nothing else would compile.

#!fsharp

let lineCharge = U.div U.coulomb U.metre
let epsilon0 = 8.8541878128e-12<F/m>

let xs = Grid.linear -1.8<m> 1.8<m> 90
let ys = Grid.linear -1.2<m> 1.2<m> 60

let potential (lambda: float<C/m>) (half: float<m>) (p: Vec2<m>) : float<V> =
    let plus = Vec2.norm { X = p.X - half; Y = p.Y }
    let minus = Vec2.norm { X = p.X + half; Y = p.Y }
    lambda / (2.0 * System.Math.PI * epsilon0) * log (minus / plus)

type Mark =
    | Heat
    | Bands
    | FieldLines

let draw (lambda: float<C/m>) (half: float<m>) (mark: Mark) =
    let phi = Field.sampleScalar xs ys (potential lambda half)

    let field =
        let g = Field.grad phi
        { g with U = Array.map (~-) g.U; W = Array.map (~-) g.W }

    // The colour scale is stated, on every mark. A live figure holds one colour scale across all
    // its states, so a stronger charge reads as a stronger potential; left to the data, that
    // scale would be set by the few grid nodes that land next to a charge.
    let stated = Chart.withColorDomain U.volt -100.0<V> 100.0<V>
    let heat = Field.heatmap U.metre U.metre U.volt phi |> stated
    let levels = [| -60.0<V>; -30.0<V>; -10.0<V>; 0.0<V>; 10.0<V>; 30.0<V>; 60.0<V> |]

    match mark with
    | Heat -> heat |> Chart.overlay (Field.contoursAt levels U.metre U.metre phi)
    | Bands -> Field.bands U.metre U.metre U.volt phi |> stated
    | FieldLines -> heat |> Chart.overlay (Field.streamlines U.metre U.metre field)
    |> Chart.withXLabel "x"
    |> Chart.withYLabel "y"

#!markdown

Three controls. The λ slider is over coulombs per metre, so the view receives a `float<C/m>`, and
its label reads `2 nC·m⁻¹`: the unit is the witness's and the prefix is chosen once, from the
range, so it does not change in the middle of a drag.

#!fsharp

let explorer =
    interact {
        let! lambda = Control.slider lineCharge "λ" 0.5e-9<C/m> 5.0e-9<C/m> 2.0e-9<C/m>
        and! half = Control.slider U.metre "d/2" 0.2<m> 1.0<m> 0.6<m>
        and! mark = Control.select "mark" [ "heatmap", Heat; "bands", Bands; "field lines", FieldLines ] 0
        return draw lambda half mark
    }

explorer

#!markdown

The figure is laid out once, over the states the controls reach at their ends, so the plot area
stays where it is while the charge grows. A zoom re-arranges it, because a zoom changes what the
axes say: zoom close to a charge and the axes, and the readout, change to millimetres.

What the cell does is a fold over the reader's messages, and it runs without a notebook. The same
zoom and the same reading, scripted:

#!fsharp

// Where a point in metres is drawn in a state, through that state's panel and its scales.
let pxAt (state: LiveState) (x: float, y: float) : Vec2<px> =
    let v = (Live.panels explorer state).[0]
    { X = v.Plot.X0 + float (v.X.Project x) * (v.Plot.X1 - v.Plot.X0)
      Y = v.Plot.Y1 - float (v.Y.Project y) * (v.Plot.Y1 - v.Plot.Y0) }

let start = Live.init explorer

// A box 24 px by 16 px around the positive charge, at x = 0.6 m.
let zoomed =
    let c = pxAt start (0.6, 0.0)
    Live.step explorer start (Zoom { X0 = c.X - 12.0<px>; Y0 = c.Y - 8.0<px>; X1 = c.X + 12.0<px>; Y1 = c.Y + 8.0<px> })

// The pointer over the charge again, where the zoomed figure draws it.
let reading = Live.step explorer zoomed (Hover(pxAt zoomed (0.6, 0.0))) |> Live.readout explorer

reading |> Option.iter (fun r -> r.Lines |> Array.iter (printfn "%s"))
printfn "zoomed onto the charge, the axes read in millimetres: %b" ((Html.widgetFigure explorer zoomed).Contains "[mm]")

#!markdown

## A hundred thousand points

A figure with more than `Html.CanvasThreshold` drawn elements, 5000, is drawn on a canvas: the
kernel sends the scene as bytes, a quarter of its SVG, and the page draws them with the same code
that draws a figure in a browser app. A slider that moves one mark sends only that mark. Set
`Html.renderMode` to `RenderMode.Svg` or `RenderMode.Canvas` to choose for every figure.

#!fsharp

let crowdTimes = Array.init 100_000 (fun i -> float i / 100_000.0 * 10.0<s>)
let crowdValues = crowdTimes |> Array.mapi (fun i t -> (sin (float t) + 0.3 * sin (float i * 12.9898)) * 1.0<m>)

let crowd =
    interact {
        let! at = Control.slider U.second "t" 1.0<s> 9.0<s> 5.0<s>
        return
            Chart.scatterIn U.second U.metre crowdTimes crowdValues
            |> Chart.addScatterIn U.second U.metre [| at |] [| sin (float at) * 1.0<m> |]
            |> Chart.withStyle (Geom.withMarkerSize 10.0<px>)
    }

printfn "drawn on a canvas: %b" (Html.drawsOnCanvas (Live.scene crowd (Live.init crowd)))

crowd

#!markdown

## Keeping what the reader did

A recorder displays as the same figure and keeps every message the reader sends, with the page's
own times. Display it, move things, then stop it.

#!fsharp

let recorder = Live.recorder explorer

recorder

#!fsharp

let session = Recorder.stop recorder
let replay = Live.film explorer (Live.record explorer session)

// The session as data, and the film as a page that plays in any browser.
System.IO.File.WriteAllText("session.json", LiveLog.toJson session)
System.IO.File.WriteAllText("replay.html", Html.film replay)

printfn "%d messages, replayed as %g s" session.Events.Length (float replay.Duration)

replay
